# Fruit-drop onset was significantly influenced by temperature, precipitation, site, and their interactions. Temperature exerted a strong negative effect on onset timing (β = −112.39 ± 13.07 SE, *P* < 0.001), indicating that warmer conditions were associated with earlier fruit-drop onset. Similarly, precipitation had a significant negative effect on onset timing (β = −55.09 ± 12.25 SE, *P* < 0.001), suggesting that wetter conditions also advanced the initiation of fruit drop. Independent of climatic effects, fruit-drop onset occurred significantly later in Yangambi than in Luki (β = 168.84 ± 18.87 SE, *P* < 0.001).

# The significant temperature × precipitation interaction (β = −60.87 ± 17.54 SE, *P* < 0.001) indicates that the influence of temperature on fruit-drop onset depended on precipitation levels, with warmer and wetter conditions jointly promoting earlier onset. Furthermore, the temperature × site interaction (β = 102.25 ± 16.20 SE, *P* < 0.001) and precipitation × site interaction (β = 38.28 ± 17.62 SE, *P* = 0.030) demonstrate that the effects of both climatic variables differed between the two forests. Specifically, the advancing effect of temperature was substantially stronger in Luki than in Yangambi, while the effect of precipitation was also reduced in Yangambi relative to Luki.

# Importantly, the significant three-way interaction between temperature, precipitation, and site (β = 70.28 ± 18.66 SE, *P* < 0.001) reveals that the combined effects of climatic drivers on fruit-drop onset varied spatially. In Luki, warm and wet conditions strongly advanced fruit-drop onset, whereas in Yangambi the synergistic effect of temperature and precipitation was considerably weaker. These findings indicate that fruit-drop phenology is highly sensitive to climatic variation, but that the magnitude and direction of climate responses differ between sites, suggesting that local environmental conditions and community composition modulate phenological responses to climate.

# Because temperature and precipitation were standardized prior to analysis, the estimated coefficients represent changes in fruit-drop onset associated with a one-standard-deviation increase in each climatic variable. At average precipitation levels, a one-standard-deviation increase in temperature advanced fruit-drop onset by approximately 112 days in Luki, compared with only about 10 days in Yangambi. Likewise, at average temperature, a one-standard-deviation increase in precipitation advanced fruit-drop onset by approximately 55 days in Luki but only about 17 days in Yangambi.




